<div class="grid-container">
  <article class="usa-prose page margin-top-6 margin-bottom-6">
    <h1>Sign the letter</h1>

    <p>
      Anyone can sign, as themselves. You do not need to have worked on USWDS.
    </p>

    <div class="usa-alert usa-alert--info margin-y-4">
      <div class="usa-alert__body">
        <h2 class="usa-alert__heading">Sign as yourself, not your employer</h2>
        <p class="usa-alert__text">
          Adding your name does not mean your current or former employer endorses
          this letter. If that distinction matters for your situation, put it in
          your role line, for example "Product designer, former 18F".
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Sign it with an npm command</h2>

    <p>Clone the repository, install dependencies, then run:</p>

    <pre class="page__code"><code>npm run sign -- --name "Your Name" --role "Product designer, former 18F"</code></pre>

    <p>Only <code>--name</code> is required. The other flag is optional:</p>

    <ul class="usa-list">
      <li><code>--role</code> your role, your tie to this work, or both, if you want it listed</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The script inserts your entry in alphabetical order, then opens a pull
      request. It never touches the letter text.
    </p>

    <h2>Sign without the command line</h2>

    <p>
      Signatures are a plain YAML file in a public repository. You can edit it
      directly and open a pull request in a browser. The rules CI enforces are
      short:
    </p>

    <ul class="usa-list">
      <li><code>name</code> is required.</li>
      <li><code>date</code> is required, in <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> format.</li>
      <li><code>role</code>, if present, is freeform: your role, your tie to this
        work, or both.</li>
      <li>No two entries may share the same name and role.</li>
      <li>Keep the list in alphabetical order by name.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Run <code>npm run lint:signatories</code> locally to check your edit before
      you push it.
    </p>

   <h2>Sign it with a Google Form</h2>

    <p>
        Add better text here:
        https://forms.gle/2yGja1JoLcEUuQCd7
    </p>

    <h2>What happens next</h2>

    <p>
      A maintainer reviews the pull request. Once it merges, the site rebuilds and
      your name appears on the <a class="usa-link" href="/">letter page</a> and in
      the <a class="usa-link" href="/archive.json">JSON archive</a>. There is no
      mailing list and no notification; the public record is the record.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
